**What changed:** The `VERSION` export from the `@angular/upgrade` package entry point (`packages/upgrade/public_api.ts`, re-exported as `export {VERSION} from './src/common/src/version';`) is marked `@deprecated` in Angular 21.1.0. Users should import `VERSION` from `@angular/upgrade/static` instead. The deprecation notice added to the module JSDoc reads: "Use the entry from `@angular/upgrade/static` instead." (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.1.x/CHANGELOG.md, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/75fe8f8af9488bae6f7068b64d44500643c5d63f.patch, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.1.x/packages/upgrade/public_api.ts)

**Affects:** Applications that import `VERSION` directly from `@angular/upgrade` (i.e. `import {VERSION} from '@angular/upgrade';`). Code that already imports it from `@angular/upgrade/static` is unaffected.

**Action:** required: change imports of `VERSION` from `@angular/upgrade` to `@angular/upgrade/static`. The deprecation only adds a JSDoc `@deprecated` tag and an editor/linter warning; no runtime behaviour changes in this minor version, so no build/dependency change is required beyond the import path.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
import {VERSION} from '@angular/upgrade';

// After
import {VERSION} from '@angular/upgrade/static';
```

**What changed:** The built-in `NgOptimizedImage` Cloudflare and Cloudinary image loaders now accept a `transform` key in `loaderParams`, appending arbitrary transformation parameters to the generated URL. In `packages/common/src/directives/ng_optimized_image/image_loaders/cloudflare_loader.ts`, `config.loaderParams?.['transform']` is normalized with `'='` (Cloudflare) and appended to the `/cdn-cgi/image/...` params; in `cloudinary_loader.ts` it is normalized with `'_'` (Cloudinary) and appended to the `/image/upload/...` params. A new helper `normalizeLoaderTransform(transform, separator)` in `normalized_options.ts` accepts either a string (passed through verbatim) or a `Record<string, string>` object, joined as `key<separator>value` pairs with commas. Transform params are combined with existing width/quality/`rounded` params, e.g. `format=auto,width=400,fit=crop,gravity=face` and `f_auto,q_auto,w_500,q_80,e_sharpen` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d8790972bea4c59a208219dd36d158b5d7e4fdde). This addresses issue #65191 ("Cloudflare loader — height-aware srcset (fit=crop)"), which requested height/`fit`/`gravity` support because the loader previously generated width-only URLs (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/65191).

**Affects:** Applications using `provideCloudflareLoader` or `provideCloudinaryLoader` with `NgOptimizedImage` that want custom transformations (e.g. `fit=crop`, `height`, `gravity`, blur, grayscale). Purely additive — no behavior changes for existing apps that do not set `transform`.

**Action:** none (optional adoption): pass `transform` via the `ngSrc`/loader params, e.g. `[loaderParams]="{ transform: { fit: 'crop', gravity: 'face', height: 300 } }"` or a string like `'e_grayscale,r_10'`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Cloudflare loader, new capability (from image_loader_spec.ts)
loader({
  src: 'img.png',
  width: 400,
  loaderParams: {transform: {fit: 'crop', gravity: 'face'}},
});
// => ''

// Cloudinary loader, new capability
loader({src: '/img.png', loaderParams: {transform: 'e_grayscale,r_10'}});
// => ''
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.1.0 adds a `transform` loader parameter to the built-in `NgOptimizedImage` loaders `provideImgixLoader` and `provideImageKitLoader` (packages/common/src/directives/ng_optimized_image/image_loaders/imgix_loader.ts, imagekit_loader.ts). A `transform` key inside `loaderParams` is now accepted and may be either a string (passed through verbatim) or a `Record<string, string>` converted by the internal `normalizeLoaderTransform(transform, separator)` helper, which joins entries with `,` using a key/value separator: `=` for Imgix (also Cloudflare), `-` for ImageKit (`_` for Cloudinary) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a6b8cb68afaded6999ee68f495512be1a9932ae4). For Imgix the transform entries are appended as extra query parameters alongside `auto=format`, `w=`, and placeholder `q=`; the URL is now built by joining a params array with `&` into `url.search` instead of using `url.searchParams.set`. For ImageKit the entries are appended to the `tr:` path segment, e.g. `${path}/tr:w-500,q-20,e-grayscale/img.png` (patch test expectations). The JSDoc for `normalizeLoaderTransform` was updated to "('_' for Cloudinary, '=' for Cloudflare/Imgix , '-' for ImageKit)".

**Affects:** Applications using `provideImgixLoader` or `provideImageKitLoader` with `NgOptimizedImage` that want extra CDN transformation parameters (or that already pass a `transform` key in `loaderParams`, which was previously ignored). No change for apps that do not set `transform`.

**Action:** none — purely additive; opt in by adding `transform` to the loader params, e.g. `provideImgixLoader('', {transform: {bri: '50', con: '20'}})` or ImageKit-style `{transform: {bl: '10', ar: '16-9', q: '80'}}`. Re-verify generated Imgix image URLs, since query parameters are now serialized via `url.search` rather than `searchParams.set`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Imgix (after 21.1.0) — imgix_loader.ts
if (config.loaderParams?.['transform']) {
  const transform = normalizeLoaderTransform(config.loaderParams['transform'], '=').split(',');
  params.push(...transform);
}
const url = new URL(`${path}/${config.src}`);
url.search = params.join('&');

// loader usage example (from image_loader_spec.ts)
loader({src: '/img.png', loaderParams: {transform: {bri: '50', con: '20', sat: '-20'}}});
// =>

// ImageKit example
loader({src: 'img.png', loaderParams: {transform: {bl: '10', ar: '16-9', q: '80'}}});
// =>
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.1.0 adds support for multiple case conditions on a single `@switch` branch in the built-in control flow: consecutive `@case` blocks can now be stacked without a body between them, so `@case (0) @case (1) { ... }` renders the same template when the switch expression `===` matches either value (previously only one `@case` per block was allowed, and the parser reported the second `@case` as unexpected) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/640693da8e667c015662246152236585d9b24e7f). `@default` can also be preceded by `@case` statements (e.g. `@case (3) @default { ... }`), and `@switch` still has no fallthrough (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/640693da8e667c015662246152236585d9b24e7f/tools/manual_api_docs/blocks/switch.md). Internally this is a compiler AST breaking change for tools: `SwitchBlock` now exposes `groups: SwitchBlockCaseGroup[]` instead of `cases`, a new `SwitchBlockCaseGroup` class (with `cases: SwitchBlockCase[]` and `children`) was added to `@angular/compiler`'s `r3_ast`, with matching TmplAst types `TmplAstSwitchBlockCaseGroup`, and a new visitor method `visitSwitchBlockCaseGroup` (commit diff above). Generated code emits a single template with a chained `===` conditional, e.g. `$MyApp_contFlowTmp$ === 0 ? 2 : $MyApp_contFlowTmp$ === 1 ? 2 : ...` (compliance golden `switch_multiple_cases_template.js`).

**Affects:** Applications that previously wrote duplicated `@case` blocks (templates parse more permissively now, but behavior of stacked cases is new); tools/ libraries that consume `@angular/compiler`'s `SwitchBlock` AST or implement a `Visitor` over template ASTs (language services, schematics, codemods) — they must handle `SwitchBlockCaseGroup` / `visitSwitchBlockCaseGroup` and the `groups` property replacing `cases`. The `NgSwitch`/`ngSwitchCase` structural directive is unchanged.

**Action:** none for applications (this is an additive template-syntax feature; existing single-`@case` templates compile identically). Required only for tooling built on the private `@angular/compiler` AST: re-verify visitor implementations handle `visitSwitchBlockCaseGroup` and read `SwitchBlock.groups` / `SwitchBlockCaseGroup.cases`.

**Before/after:**
```angular-html
<!-- Before: duplicated bodies required -->
@switch (condition) {
  @case ('reviewer') { <app-reviewer-dashboard /> }
  @case ('editor') { <app-editor-dashboard /> }
}

<!-- After (21.1): consecutive @case share one block -->
@switch (condition) {
  @case ('reviewer')
  @case ('editor') {
    <app-editor-dashboard />
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler now supports empty `@case` blocks in the built-in `@switch` control flow. Previously (21.0.x), a `@case` written with an empty body, e.g. `@case (-1) {}`, was treated by `createSwitchBlock` in `packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_control_flow.ts` as a "case without body" and therefore merged as a *consecutive/fall-through* case with the following `@case` — so `@case (-1) {}` followed by `@case (0) @case(1) {...}` made `-1` render the `0`/`1` branch. The fix in `packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_control_flow.ts` refines the `caseWithoutBody` check to `node.children.length === 0 && node.endSourceSpan !== null && node.endSourceSpan.start.offset === node.endSourceSpan.end.offset`, so a `{}` empty body is its own branch: it now emits its own (empty) template in the `ɵɵconditionalCreate` chain and `ɵɵconditional` matches it as a distinct branch index instead of sharing the next case's index (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0ad3adc7c6d4094f1e3432a3f2e3bdc9862cb4fa). A matching `HtmlParser` spec ("should parse empty cases in a switch block") and a `@switch` acceptance test ("should support an empty case block", expecting empty text content for the empty case) were added in the same commit.

**Affects:** Applications/templates that use the built-in `@switch` control flow with an explicitly empty `@case (...) {}` block (a case whose body is `{}`); templates that only use valueless fall-through `@case (0) @case(1) { ... }` are unaffected.

**Action:** none — templates keep the same source form; only the compiled/rendered behaviour of an empty `@case (...) {}` changes (it now renders nothing instead of the next case's content). Applications that relied on the old fall-through rendering for an empty `@case` must re-verify those `@switch` blocks and add the intended content to the case body if fall-through output was the previous behaviour.

**Before/after:**
```
@switch (value()) {
  @case (-1) {}
  @case (0) @case(1) {
    case 01
  }
}
```
```
// before: empty @case shared the next case's branch
$r3$.ɵɵconditional(($MyApp_contFlowTmp$ = ctx.value()) === 0 ? 2 : $MyApp_contFlowTmp$ === 1 ? 2 : $MyApp_contFlowTmp$ === 2 ? 3 : 4);
// after: empty @case is its own branch
$r3$.ɵɵconditional((tmp_1_0 = ctx.value()) === -1 ? 2 : tmp_1_0 === 0 ? 3 : tmp_1_0 === 1 ? 3 : tmp_1_0 === 2 ? 4 : 5);
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.1 adds a new public API `provideStabilityDebugging(): EnvironmentProviders` exported from `@angular/core`, which logs why the application has not stabilized if `ApplicationRef`/`ApplicationRef.whenStable()` does not settle within 9 seconds (`STABILITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = APPLICATION_IS_STABLE_TIMEOUT - 1_000`, where the previously-private `APPLICATION_IS_STABLE_TIMEOUT = 10_000` in `packages/core/src/hydration/api.ts` is now exported) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/99ad18a4ee82ecc5524106d1d403ccfa9bae2304). It registers a `DEBUG_TASK_TRACKER` `InjectionToken`/`DebugTaskTracker` (`add(taskId)`/`remove(taskId)`) that `PendingTasksInternal` now calls, so the console shows the creation stacks of `PendingTasks` keeping the app unstable; with Zone.js it also reports `TaskTrackingZone` macrotask stacks if `zone.js/plugins/task-tracking` is imported. `provideClientHydration` (platform-browser) now includes `provideStabilityDebugging()` automatically in dev mode (`ngDevMode`) only. Added to the bundle golden symbols (`APPLICATION_IS_STABLE_TIMEOUT`, `DEBUG_TASK_TRACKER`, `STABILITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD`). Motivation: NG0506 hydration warning gave no insight into what blocked stability (#52912).

**Affects:** Applications using `provideClientHydration()` get extra dev-mode console diagnostics automatically; only apps that add `provideStabilityDebugging()` (or the zone plugin) opt in deliberately. No breaking behaviour change.

**Action:** none — purely additive. Optionally, to debug an unstable/hydrating app, add `provideStabilityDebugging()` to `bootstrapApplication` providers (needed for production bundles or SSR without hydration) and import `zone.js/plugins/task-tracking`; both must be removed afterwards because Angular does not tree-shake them out of production bundles.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import {provideStabilityDebugging} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import 'zone.js/plugins/task-tracking'; // Use if you have Zone.js with `provideZoneChangeDetection`

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [provideStabilityDebugging()],
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.1.0 added support for rest/spread arguments (`...` operator) in template expression function calls. Previously the `...` operator in templates was only parsed inside array literals; `_ParseAST.parseCallArguments` in `packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts` now calls a new shared `parseSpreadElement()` helper for arguments starting with `...`, producing a `SpreadElement` AST node (with proper `span`/`sourceSpan`, e.g. `[argumentSpan] 1, ...foo`). This works in regular and safe-navigated calls (`fn(...foo)`, `fn?.(1, ...foo, 2)`) and template type checking now type-checks rest arguments (a mismatch reports e.g. `Argument of type 'number' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'.`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a0dfa5fa86f40520b0e368a021b3c72009a45e8e; listed under `core`/feat in the 21.1.0 changelog, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.1.x/CHANGELOG.md). Emitted code uses native spread, e.g. `ctx.fn(...ctx.foo)` inside `ɵɵtextInterpolate1`; nested spreads become pure functions (`ɵɵpureFunction1(5, $c1$, ...)`).

**Affects:** Nothing existing — previously `fn(...foo)` in a template was a template parse error, so only applications that want to newly write rest arguments in template expressions. No existing behaviour changes.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```angular-html
<!-- 21.1.0+: rest arguments in template function calls (from call_rest.ts) -->
{{fn(...foo)}}
{{fn(1, ...foo, 2)}}
{{fn(...foo, 1, ...bar, ...baz, 2)}}
{{fn(1, ...[2, ...[3]])}}
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.1 expands the template expression parser to allow spread elements inside array literals. Previously only object literals supported `...` spread; now array literals do too, parsed by a new `parseLiteralArray` in `packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts` producing a new `SpreadElement` AST node, converted at compile time to `o.SpreadElementExpr` and compiled through the pure-expression machinery into `ɵɵpureFunction1/2/3…` calls with `...PureFunctionParameterExpr` spreads (e.g. `@let simple = [...foo];` compiles to `$c0$ = a0 => [...a0]` used via `ɵɵpureFunction1`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/6e18fa8bc9d7e6801e2e89e635c2f759dc422317). Supported forms include `[...foo]`, `[1, ...foo, 2]`, `[...foo, 1, ...bar, ...baz, 2]`, and nested inline arrays `[1, ...[2, ...[3]]]`. Template type-checking also understands spreads, e.g. `[1, ...foo]` with `foo = ['two']` is typed `(string | number)[]` and yields `Argument of type '(string | number)[]' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number[]'` when misused.

**Affects:** Applications that want to use `...` inside template array literals (`@let` declarations, interpolations, or bindings); existing templates that did not use array spread are unaffected — this only removes a previously invalid-syntax restriction.

**Action:** none — purely additive syntax support; no code, config, or dependency changes are required when upgrading. Optionally re-verify any template that previously had to avoid array spread as a workaround.

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- New in 21.1: spread elements in template array literals -->
@let simple = [...foo];
@let otherEntries = [1, ...foo, 2];
@let multipleSpreads = [...foo, 1, ...bar, ...baz, 2];
@let inlineArraySpread = [1, ...[2, ...[3]]];

{{simple}} {{otherEntries}} {{multipleSpreads}} {{inlineArraySpread}}
```

**What changed:** The Angular template expression parser now supports spread (`...`) expressions inside object literals in templates, e.g. `@let obj = {a: 1, ...foo, b: 2}` (added in `packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts` via new AST key kinds `LiteralMapSpreadKey` / `LiteralMapPropertyKey` alongside the existing `LiteralMapKey`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e407280ab53cde5f93c3a643457c848845c6ec8b). Spreads work in `@let` declarations, interpolations, and the commit notes this is "handy when constructing maps for `class` bindings". Generated code compiles spreads to pure functions (`ɵɵpureFunction0/1/3`), so values are recomputed only when inputs change; template type-checking understands spreads (e.g. diagnostic `Argument of type '{ b: string; a: number; }' is not assignable to parameter of type '{ a: number; b: number; }'`) (commit patch).

**Affects:** Nothing for existing apps — purely additive; templates previously failed to parse with a template parse error if they used `...` in object literals. Only applications that want this new syntax are affected.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```angular-html
<!-- Before 21.1: template parse error. From 21.1: valid -->
@let simple = {...foo};
@let otherProps = {a: 1, ...foo, b: 2};
@let multipleSpreads = {...foo, a: 1, ...bar, ...baz, b: 2};
@let objectLiteral = {a: 1, ...{b: {...{c: 3}}}};

{{simple}} {{otherProps}} {{multipleSpreads}} {{objectLiteral}}
```

